# MS Office and Keyboard Shortcuts for Exams

> Almost every SSC, HSSC CET and police paper asks about Word, Excel, PowerPoint,
> file extensions or a keyboard shortcut. The questions are mechanical: "Which key
> starts a slide show?", "What is the extension of an Excel file?", "Which function
> adds a range?". Learn the tables below and these become the easiest marks in
> computer awareness.

## 1. Microsoft Office at a Glance

**MS Office** is a suite of application software from Microsoft. The main members:

| Program | Used for | Default extension (2007 onward) | Older extension |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Word | Documents, letters, reports (word processor) | .docx | .doc |
| Excel | Spreadsheets, calculations, charts | .xlsx | .xls |
| PowerPoint | Presentations (slides) | .pptx | .ppt |
| Access | Database | .accdb | .mdb |
| Outlook | Email and calendar | .pst (data file) | |

The **Ribbon** is the strip of tabs and command groups at the top (introduced in Office 2007). The **Quick Access Toolbar** holds Save, Undo and Redo. The **Backstage view** (the File tab) holds Save, Print and Options. The **status bar** at the bottom shows page number, word count and zoom.

## 2. MS Word

| Tab | Typical commands |
| --- | --- |
| Home | Font, paragraph, alignment, styles, find and replace |
| Insert | Table, picture, shapes, header and footer, page number, hyperlink, WordArt |
| Layout (Page Layout in older versions) | Margins, orientation, size, columns |
| References | Table of contents, footnotes, citations |
| Mailings | Mail merge, envelopes and labels |
| Review | Spelling and grammar, comments, track changes |
| View | Zoom, ruler, print layout, web layout |

- **Mail merge** produces many personalised letters from one main document and a data source.
- **Header** appears at the top margin and **footer** at the bottom of each page.
- **Orientation:** portrait (vertical) or landscape (horizontal).
- **Alignment:** left, centre, right, justify.
- **Format Painter** copies formatting from one text to another.
- **Thesaurus** gives synonyms; **Track Changes** records edits.
- A **template** is a ready-made document design with extension .dotx.

## 3. MS Excel

An Excel file is a **workbook**; each page in it is a **worksheet**. A worksheet has **1,048,576 rows** and **16,384 columns** (the last column is **XFD**) in versions from 2007 onward. The intersection of a row and column is a **cell**, named like **B5** (column then row). The **active cell** is the one selected; the **Name Box** shows its address and the **Formula Bar** shows its contents. A group of cells is a **range**, written **A1:C4**.

A formula always starts with the **equals sign (=)**. By default, text aligns to the left and numbers to the right.

| Function | Result |
| --- | --- |
| SUM | Adds the numbers in a range |
| AVERAGE | Arithmetic mean |
| MAX and MIN | Largest and smallest value |
| COUNT | Counts cells holding numbers |
| COUNTA | Counts cells that are not empty |
| IF | Returns one value if a test is true, another if false |
| VLOOKUP and HLOOKUP | Look up a value in a column (vertical) or row (horizontal) |

**Cell references:** relative (A1), absolute (**$A$1**, fixed when copied) and mixed (A$1 or $A1). Pressing **F4** while editing a formula cycles through them.

**Common error values:** **#DIV/0!** (division by zero), **#NAME?** (unknown function or name), **#VALUE!** (wrong type of argument), **#REF!** (invalid cell reference) and **####** (column too narrow to show the value).

Other features: **Sort** (ascending or descending), **Filter** (show only matching rows), **Chart** (column, bar, line, pie), **Pivot Table** (summarise data), **Freeze Panes** (keep headings visible) and **Merge and Center**.

[example]
Cells A1 to A5 hold 12, 8, 15, 5 and 10. The formula =SUM(A1:A5) gives 50, =AVERAGE(A1:A5) gives 10, =MAX(A1:A5) gives 15 and =MIN(A1:A5) gives 5. A range of A1:C4 contains 3 columns x 4 rows = 12 cells.
[/example]

## 4. MS PowerPoint

A presentation is made of **slides**. Important ideas:

- **Views:** Normal, Slide Sorter (rearranging slides), Reading View and Slide Show.
- **Transitions** control how one slide changes to the next; **Animations** control how objects on a slide move or appear. This pair is a classic trap.
- **Slide Master** sets the common design for all slides; a **theme** is a set of colours, fonts and effects; a **layout** arranges placeholders.
- **Notes** hold speaker comments. A **handout** is a printed set of slides.
- **Extensions:** .pptx (presentation), **.ppsx** (opens directly as a slide show) and **.potx** (template).
- Slide Show tab: start from the beginning or from the current slide, rehearse timings, set up show.

## 5. Keyboard Shortcuts That Are Asked

### General (Word and most Windows programs)

| Keys | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Ctrl + C, X, V | Copy, cut, paste |
| Ctrl + Z, Y | Undo, redo |
| Ctrl + A | Select all |
| Ctrl + B, I, U | Bold, italic, underline |
| Ctrl + S | Save |
| F12 | Save As (in Office) |
| Ctrl + P | Print |
| Ctrl + F | Find |
| Ctrl + H | Replace |
| Ctrl + K | Insert hyperlink |
| Ctrl + E, L, R, J | Centre, left, right, justify |
| Ctrl + Enter | Page break |
| Ctrl + Home, End | Start, end of document |
| F7 | Spelling and grammar check |
| Shift + F3 | Change case |

### Excel and PowerPoint

| Keys | Action |
| --- | --- |
| F2 | Edit the active cell |
| F4 | Toggle absolute reference (in Excel) |
| Alt + = | AutoSum |
| Ctrl + ; | Insert today's date |
| Ctrl + Shift + ; | Insert current time |
| Alt + Enter | New line inside a cell |
| Ctrl + Space | Select entire column |
| Shift + Space | Select entire row |
| F5 (PowerPoint) | Start slide show from the beginning |
| Shift + F5 (PowerPoint) | Start from the current slide |
| Ctrl + M (PowerPoint) | Insert a new slide |
| Esc (PowerPoint) | End the slide show |

### Windows desktop

| Keys | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Alt + Tab | Switch between open windows |
| Alt + F4 | Close the active window |
| Windows + D | Show desktop |
| Windows + E | Open File Explorer |
| Windows + L | Lock the computer |
| Ctrl + Shift + Esc | Open Task Manager |
| Ctrl + Alt + Del | Security options screen |
| Shift + Delete | Delete a file permanently (skips Recycle Bin) |
| F2 | Rename a selected file |

## 6. Common File Extensions

| Type | Extensions |
| --- | --- |
| Text and documents | .txt, .docx, .pdf, .rtf |
| Images | .jpg or .jpeg, .png, .gif, .bmp |
| Audio and video | .mp3, .wav, .mp4, .avi |
| Programs | .exe |
| Compressed | .zip, .rar |
| Web | .html, .htm |
| Data | .csv (comma separated values), .xlsx |

**Traps:** Ctrl + F is Find but Ctrl + H is Replace; transition is not animation; COUNT counts only numbers while COUNTA counts any non-empty cell; the Excel limit of 1,048,576 rows applies to 2007 and later (the old .xls had 65,536).

[summary]
Word, Excel and PowerPoint save as .docx, .xlsx and .pptx (older .doc, .xls, .ppt). Excel has 1,048,576 rows and 16,384 columns (XFD); formulas start with = and key functions are SUM, AVERAGE, MAX, MIN, COUNT, COUNTA, IF and VLOOKUP. PowerPoint: F5 starts the show, Shift + F5 starts from the current slide, transitions differ from animations. Memorise Ctrl shortcuts (C, X, V, Z, Y, A, B, I, U, S, P, F, H, E, L, R, J), Alt + Tab, Alt + F4, Windows + D and Shift + Delete.
[/summary]
